WEBVTT

00:00.550 --> 00:08.690
A supplier identity appears in an external report. If someone using that role reached the finance boundary, would the on-call analyst know?

00:08.930 --> 00:11.565
This is a simulated ServiceWatch case.

00:12.900 --> 00:18.415
The second mention repeats the first. An analyst links the duplicate and keeps one unconfirmed lead.

00:18.655 --> 00:22.690
The identity team still has to verify whether the account was affected.

00:24.000 --> 00:31.495
The analyst reviews a synthetic ARES campaign export. The exercise uses a test identity with the supplier role.

00:31.735 --> 00:38.870
Two criteria matter. Finance access must be blocked, and the alert must reach the on-call queue within five minutes.

00:40.200 --> 00:47.305
The simulated Infection Monkey report shows the expected supplier portal responding, while finance is unreachable.

00:47.545 --> 00:52.435
A matching firewall denial supports the conclusion that this boundary blocked the test.

00:53.750 --> 00:59.660
The firewall logged the block at ten. Eight seconds later, the detection system created an alert.

00:59.900 --> 01:05.115
At ten oh five the on-call queue was still empty. The alert had been routed to the wrong queue.

01:06.450 --> 01:12.235
After the routing correction, the analyst repeats the same check. Finance remains blocked.

01:12.475 --> 01:18.080
This time the on-call queue receives the alert in forty-two seconds, inside the five-minute window.

01:19.400 --> 01:26.825
The review can now close both control checks for this path. The identity team keeps the original exposure lead open.

01:27.065 --> 01:32.485
That distinction tells the customer what was demonstrated, and what still needs investigation.
